.B \fB7\fP
Show/hide NPU stats
.TP
.B \fB8\fP
Show/hide MPP stats
.TP
.B \fB/\fP
Switch between process command line or command name
.TP
.B \fBF5\fP or \fBCTRL\-R\fP
Refresh user interface
.TP
.B \fBSHIFT\-LEFT\fP
Navigation left through the process sort
.TP
.B \fBSHIFT\-RIGHT\fP
Navigation right through the process sort
.TP
.B \fBLEFT\fP
Scroll the selected process command line left
.TP
.B \fBRIGHT\fP
Scroll the selected process command line right
.TP
.B \fBUP\fP
Up in the processes list
.TP
.B \fBDOWN\fP
Down in the processes list
.UNINDENT
.sp
With \fB\-\-arrow\-keys\-sort\fP, \fBLEFT\fP/\fBRIGHT\fP and \fBSHIFT\-LEFT\fP/\fBSHIFT\-RIGHT\fP
are swapped. With \fB\-\-disable\-cursor\fP, the keys acting on the selected process
(\fBENTER\fP, \fBk\fP, \fB+\fP, \fB\-\fP, \fBUP\fP, \fBDOWN\fP, command line scrolling) are
disabled.
.sp
In the Glances client browser (accessible through the \fB\-\-browser\fP
command line argument):
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBENTER\fP
Run the selected server
.TP
.B \fBUP\fP
Up in the servers list
.TP
.B \fBDOWN\fP
Down in the servers list
.TP
.B \fB1\fP
Show the servers in the configured order
.TP
.B \fB2\fP
Sort the servers by status (UNKNOWN > OFFLINE > PROTECTED > UNSUPPORTED > ONLINE)
.TP
.B \fB3\fP
Sort the servers by status, reversed (ONLINE first)
.TP
.B \fBq|ESC\fP
Quit Glances
.UNINDENT
.SH CONFIGURATION
.sp
Glances reads its configuration from a single \fBglances.conf\fP file, laid
over built\-in defaults and overridden by environment variables.
.SH LOCATION
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
A template is available in the \fB/usr{,/local}/share/doc/glances\fP
(Unix\-like) directory or directly on GitHub \%<https://\:raw\:.githubusercontent\:.com/\:nicolargo/\:glances/\:master/\:conf/\:glances\:.conf>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Glances loads \fBexactly one\fP configuration file:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
the file given with the \fB\-C <path>\fP (\fB\-\-config\fP) command line option.
No other file is searched: when it does not exist, Glances logs a warning
and runs on its built\-in defaults;
.IP 2. 3
otherwise, the first existing file of:
.INDENT 3.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glances/glances.conf\fP (\fB~/.config/glances/glances.conf\fP
when \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP is not set);
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/etc/glances/glances.conf\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Files are not merged: a user file hides the system\-wide one entirely. Running
without any configuration file is valid, the built\-in defaults are used.
.sp
A file that exists but cannot be read or parsed stops Glances: it is never
skipped silently (it could hold the \fB[outputs] password\fP).
.sp
Any option can then be overridden with an environment variable named
\fBGLANCES_<SECTION>__<KEY>\fP (note the double underscore), for example
\fBGLANCES_OUTPUTS__BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0\fP\&. This is handy in containers.
Options given on the command line override both.
.sp
\fBglances \-\-issue\fP shows which configuration file was loaded.
.SH SYNTAX
.sp
Glances read configuration files in the \fIini\fP syntax.
.sp
The file is segmented by sections composed of key/value pairs.
.sp
For example “refresh=2” is a key/value pair. refresh is the key and 2 is the value.
.sp
Key is always a string.
.sp
Value is one of the followings types:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
integer (ex: refresh=2)
.IP \(bu 2
float (ex: foo=1.23)
.IP \(bu 2
bool (ex: disable=true). Glances accepts true, 1, yes, on (and false, 0,
no, off) as bool.
.IP \(bu 2
comma\-separated list of values (ex: focus=.*firefox.*,.*python.*)
.UNINDENT
.sp
Values are used as written: shell commands between backticks are not
executed (Glances 3 and 4 “dynamic values” are gone).
.sp
A first section (called global) is available:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[global]
# Refresh rate (default is a minimum of 2 seconds)
# Can be overwritten by the \-t <sec> option
# It is also possible to overwrite it in each plugin section
refresh=2
# History size (maximum number of values)
# Default is 1200 values (~1h with the default refresh rate)
# 0 disables the history (same as \-\-disable\-history).
history_size=1200
# Set the way Glances should display the date (default is %Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %Z)
#strftime_format=%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %Z
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
than a second one concerning the user interface:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[outputs]
# Options for all UIs
#\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
# Separator in the Curses interface (between top and others plugins)
separator=True
# Limit the number of processes to display (in the WebUI)
max_processes_display=25
# Plugin title colour in the Curses interface, tuned for your terminal
# background. \(dqdark\(dq (default) is bold white, suited to the dark
# backgrounds most terminals use; \(dqlight\(dq is a dark grey for a white
# background. No single colour is legible on both, hence the switch.
# The WebUI uses the same key to pick its dark or light theme.
#theme=light
# Options for the server (glances \-s / \-w)
#\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
# Address and TCP port the server listens on (\-\-bind / \-\-port)
# Default is 127.0.0.1 (loopback only) and 61208
#bind_address=127.0.0.1
#port=61208
# PBKDF2 hash of the REST API / WebUI password, printed by
# \(dqglances \-\-set\-password\(dq (never the clear password)
#password=<salt>$<pbkdf2 hex digest>
# User name expected with the password (default: glances)
#username=glances
# Serve the interactive API documentation: Swagger UI at /docs and ReDoc at
# /redoc.
# Both are unauthenticated when no password is set, like the rest of the API,
# and they describe every route \- disable them on an internet\-facing server.
# The WebUI footer links /docs only when this is enabled.
# Overridden on the command line by \-\-api\-doc / \-\-no\-api\-doc.
# Default is True
#api_doc=False
# CORS options
# Comma separated list of origins that should be permitted to make cross\-origin requests.
# Default is no list: no CORS header is sent
#cors_origins=https://dashboard.example.com
# Indicate that cookies and credentials should be supported for cross\-origin requests.
# Default is False (forced to False when cors_origins contains *)
#cors_allow_credentials=False
# Define SSL files (keyfile_password is optional) to serve HTTPS
#ssl_keyfile=./glances.local+3\-key.pem
#ssl_keyfile_password=kfp
#ssl_certfile=./glances.local+3.pem
# Rate limiting, per client address
#auth_fail_per_minute=10
#rate_limit_per_minute=0
#rate_limit_burst=0
# DNS rebinding protection (allowed HTTP Host headers)
#webui_allowed_hosts=localhost,127.0.0.1,myserver.example.com
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The server options are detailed in the next section.
.SH SERVER OPTIONS
.sp
The \fB[outputs]\fP section also configures the REST API and WebUI server
started by \fBglances \-s\fP or \fBglances \-w\fP\&.
.sp
\fBListening address.\fP \fBbind_address\fP (default \fB127.0.0.1\fP) and \fBport\fP
(default \fB61208\fP) set where the server listens; \fB\-\-bind\fP and \fB\-\-port\fP
override them. The default only accepts connections from the local machine:
set \fBbind_address=0.0.0.0\fP to serve the network (the Docker image does).
.sp
\fBPassword.\fP Glances runs without authentication by default. To protect the
API and the WebUI, generate a hash with:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ glances \-\-set\-password
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
and paste the printed value into \fB[outputs] password\fP\&. The key holds a
PBKDF2 hash (\fB<salt>$<hex digest>\fP), never the clear password: Glances
never writes to \fBglances.conf\fP itself. Hashes made by Glances 4 are not
compatible and must be generated again. The user name is \fBglances\fP unless
\fBusername\fP is set. Clients authenticate with HTTP Basic, or with a JWT
Bearer token obtained from \fB/api/5/token\fP; \fBjwt_secret_key\fP (a random
key per run when unset, so tokens do not survive a restart) and
\fBjwt_expire_minutes\fP (default \fB60\fP) configure the tokens.
.sp
\fBRate limiting.\fP Requests are counted per client address (an IPv6 client
per /64):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauth_fail_per_minute\fP (default \fB10\fP, \fB0\fP disables it): failed
authentications allowed per minute. Past the limit, the address gets HTTP
429 before its credentials are checked; requests carrying credentials
already known good are still served.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrate_limit_per_minute\fP (default \fB0\fP, off): general limit, in requests
per minute. A WebUI tab polls about 30 times a minute, a \fBglances \-c\fP
client about 90.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrate_limit_burst\fP (default \fB0\fP, meaning one minute’s worth): requests
allowed at once.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB/status\fP and \fB/healthz\fP are never limited.
.sp
\fBDNS rebinding.\fP \fBwebui_allowed_hosts\fP is the comma\-separated list of
HTTP \fBHost\fP headers the server accepts (wildcards such as
\fB*.example.com\fP are supported). When it is not set, a server bound to a
loopback address (the default) only answers \fBlocalhost\fP, \fB127.0.0.1\fP and
\fB[::1]\fP; a server bound to the network applies no host filtering and logs
a warning. Set it behind a reverse proxy that forwards its own \fBHost\fP, and
on any internet\-facing deployment. The MCP endpoint has its own list,
\fBmcp_allowed_hosts\fP\&.
.sp
\fBTLS.\fP Set both \fBssl_keyfile\fP and \fBssl_certfile\fP (and
\fBssl_keyfile_password\fP when the key is encrypted) to serve HTTPS. Setting
only one of them, or a file that cannot be read, stops the server instead of
falling back to plain HTTP.
.sp
\fBCORS.\fP No CORS header is sent unless \fBcors_origins\fP lists the origins
allowed to make cross\-origin requests (methods \fBGET\fP and \fBPOST\fP, request
headers \fBAuthorization\fP and \fBContent\-Type\fP).
\fBcors_allow_credentials\fP (default \fBFalse\fP) is ignored when the list
contains \fB*\fP, which the CORS specification forbids.
.sp
\fBConfiguration endpoint.\fP \fB/api/5/config\fP serves the configuration. When
no password is set, it only exposes the options the WebUI needs
(\fB[global] refresh\fP and \fB[outputs] theme\fP, \fBmax_processes_display\fP,
\fBapi_doc\fP). With a password, authenticated clients get the whole
configuration, secrets (passwords, tokens, keys…) redacted.
.sp
Each plugin, export module, and application monitoring process (AMP) can
have a section. Below is an example for the CPU plugin:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[cpu]
disable=False
refresh=3
user_careful=50
user_warning=70
user_critical=90
iowait_careful=50
iowait_warning=70
iowait_critical=90
system_careful=50
system_warning=70
system_critical=90
steal_careful=50
steal_warning=70
steal_critical=90
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
an InfluxDB export module:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[influxdb]
# Configuration for the \-\-export influxdb option
# https://influxdb.com/
host=localhost
port=8086
user=root
password=root
db=glances
prefix=localhost
#tags=foo:bar,spam:eggs
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or a Nginx AMP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[amp_nginx]
# Nginx status page should be enabled (https://easyengine.io/tutorials/nginx/status\-page/)
enable=true
regex=\e/usr\e/sbin\e/nginx
refresh=60
one_line=false
status_url=http://localhost/nginx_status
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH LOGGING
.sp
Glances logs all of its internal messages to a log file.
.sp
\fBDEBUG\fP messages can be logged using the \fB\-d\fP option on the command
line.
.sp
The log file is named \fBglances.log\fP\&. The file is automatically rotated when its size exceeds 1 MB.
.sp
The log file lives in a folder private to the user (\fB$XDG_CACHE_HOME/glances\fP
or \fB~/.local/share/glances\fP) and is readable by its owner only. Glances
never falls back to a shared temporary folder such as \fB/tmp\fP: when no
private folder can be created (no home directory), it does not log to a file.
.sp
If you want to use another system path or change the log message, you
can use your logger configuration. First of all, you have to create
a \fBglances.json\fP file with, for example, the following content (JSON
format):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqversion\(dq: 1,
    \(dqdisable_existing_loggers\(dq: \(dqFalse\(dq,
    \(dqroot\(dq: {
        \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqINFO\(dq,
        \(dqhandlers\(dq: [\(dqfile\(dq, \(dqconsole\(dq]
    },
    \(dqformatters\(dq: {
        \(dqstandard\(dq: {
            \(dqformat\(dq: \(dq%(asctime)s \-\- %(levelname)s \-\- %(message)s\(dq
        },
        \(dqshort\(dq: {
            \(dqformat\(dq: \(dq%(levelname)s: %(message)s\(dq
        },
        \(dqfree\(dq: {
            \(dqformat\(dq: \(dq%(message)s\(dq
        }
    },
    \(dqhandlers\(dq: {
        \(dqfile\(dq: {
            \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqDEBUG\(dq,
            \(dqclass\(dq: \(dqlogging.handlers.RotatingFileHandler\(dq,
            \(dqformatter\(dq: \(dqstandard\(dq,
            \(dqfilename\(dq: \(dq/var/tmp/glances.log\(dq
        },
        \(dqconsole\(dq: {
            \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqCRITICAL\(dq,
            \(dqclass\(dq: \(dqlogging.StreamHandler\(dq,
            \(dqformatter\(dq: \(dqfree\(dq
        }
    },
    \(dqloggers\(dq: {
        \(dqdebug\(dq: {
            \(dqhandlers\(dq: [\(dqfile\(dq, \(dqconsole\(dq],
            \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqDEBUG\(dq
        },
        \(dqverbose\(dq: {
            \(dqhandlers\(dq: [\(dqfile\(dq, \(dqconsole\(dq],
            \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqINFO\(dq
        },
        \(dqstandard\(dq: {
            \(dqhandlers\(dq: [\(dqfile\(dq],
            \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqINFO\(dq
        },
        \(dqrequests\(dq: {
            \(dqhandlers\(dq: [\(dqfile\(dq, \(dqconsole\(dq],
            \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqERROR\(dq
        },
        \(dqelasticsearch\(dq: {
            \(dqhandlers\(dq: [\(dqfile\(dq, \(dqconsole\(dq],
            \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqERROR\(dq
        },
        \(dqelasticsearch.trace\(dq: {
            \(dqhandlers\(dq: [\(dqfile\(dq, \(dqconsole\(dq],
            \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqERROR\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
and start Glances using the following command line:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
LOG_CFG=<path>/glances.json glances
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Replace \fB<path>\fP with the directory where your \fBglances.json\fP file
is hosted.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH EXAMPLES
.sp
Monitor local machine, also called standalone mode,
with the Text\-based user interface (TUI):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To monitor the local machine with the Web user interface (WebUI),
run the following command line (\fB\-w\fP is the same as \fB\-s\fP):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances \-w
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
then, open a Web Browser to \%<http://\:localhost:61208>\&.
.sp
Monitor local machine and export stats to a CSV file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances –export csv –export\-csv\-file /tmp/glances.csv
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Export stats without the TUI (for example as a system service):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances –quiet –export influxdb2
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Monitor local machine and export stats to an InfluxDB server with 5s
refresh time (also possible to export to OpenTSDB, Cassandra, Statsd,
ElasticSearch, RabbitMQ, Riemann and others, see –modules\-list):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances \-t 5 –export influxdb
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is also possible to export stats to multiple endpoints:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances \-t 5 –export influxdb,statsd,csv
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Start a Glances server (REST API on /api/5 and WebUI) listening on all
the network interfaces (the default bind address is 127.0.0.1):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances \-s \-B 0.0.0.0
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Protect the server with a password: generate a hash and set it as
\fBpassword\fP in the \fB[outputs]\fP section of glances.conf:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances –set\-password
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Connect Glances to a Glances v5 server (client mode):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances \-c <ip_server>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Connect to a password\-protected server (prompt for the password):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances \-c <ip_server> \-u glances –password
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Connect to a Glances server and export stats to a StatsD server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances \-c <ip_server> –export statsd
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Start the TUI Central Glances Browser:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances –browser
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Start the WebUI Central Glances Browser (served on /browser and
/api/5/serverslist):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances –browser \-s
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If you do not want to see the local Glances Web Server in the browser list please use –disable\-autodiscover option.
.sp
Display a neofetch\-like summary of the machine:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances –fetch
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Print a report to paste into a bug report:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ glances –issue
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
